    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="linear-operators-complete">
    <title>Linear Operators (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We've seen the defining properties of <q>linear</q>, and how linear transformations are 
                        synonymous with matrix multiplication in finite dimensions. We'll look more specifically at 
                        linear transformations in the context of quantum mechanics.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Linear Operators</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
              The terms <term>linear transformation</term> and <term>linear operator</term> are often used interchangeably. However,
              in quantum mechanics, <term>linear operator</term> is the most often used term and typically describes a 
              linear transformation <m>T</m> such that
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>T: V \to V</m>; it is a function from a vector space to itself.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    <m>V</m> is a function space (like the Hilbert space containing wave functions).
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ol>
                    </p>
                </solution>
                
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <title>Observables</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The role of linear operators in quantum mechanics is to measure the physical properties of a system
                (called <term>observables</term>). They are defined such that we can compute the expected value of
                the given observable in state <m>\psi(x)</m> using the inner product:
                
                
            </p>
            <p>
                The expected value of observable <m>A</m> in state <m>\psi(x)</m> is 
                <md>
                    \langle A \rangle := \langle \psi(x), \hat{A}[\psi(x)] \rangle = \int_{-\infty}^\infty \overline{\psi(x)}\cdot \hat{A}[\psi(x)] \quad dx
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Position Operator.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The position operator is <m>\hat{x}: V \to V</m>, <m>\hat{x}[\psi(x)] = x \psi(x)</m>. See class notes to verify
                    that this is a linear operator. Also, note that the expected value of position is consistent with what we 
                    defined earlier in the <url href="https://jshriner6.github.io/math271-FA2026/course/normal-expectation-complete.html">Normalization and Expectation section</url>:
                    <md>
                        \langle x \rangle = \int_{-\infty}^\infty \overline{\psi(x)}x\psi(x) \quad dx = \int_{-\infty}^\infty x|\psi(x)|^2 \quad dx
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Momentum Operator.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    The momentum operator is <m>\hat{p}: V \to V</m>, <m>\hat{p}[\psi(x)] = -i\overline{h}\frac{d\psi}{dx}</m>. See
                    notes from class to verify that this is a linear operator. Also, we can compute the expected value of 
                    momentum by
                    <md>
                        \langle p \rangle = \langle \psi(x), \hat{p}[\psi(x)] \rangle = \int_{-\infty}^\infty \overline{\psi(x)}\cdot(-i\overline{h}\frac{d\psi}{dx}) \quad dx
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Entergy Operator.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    This operator is also known as the <term>Hamiltonian</term> operator, and is the operator
                    <m>\hat{H}: V \to V</m>, <m>\hat{H}[\psi(x)]= \frac{-\overline{h}^2}{2m}\frac{d^2\psi}{dx^2} + V(x)\psi(x)</m>.
                    Physically, this is the sum of kinetic and potential energies. See notes from class to verify this is a linear operator.
                    Also, we can compute the expected value of 
                    energy by
                    <md>
                        \langle H \rangle = \langle \psi(x),\hat{H}[\psi(x)] \rangle = \int_{-\infty}^\infty \overline{\psi(x)} \cdot \left( \frac{-\overline{h}^2}{2m}\frac{d^2\psi}{dx^2} + V(x)\psi(x)\right) \quad dx
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        
    </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
    <title>Self-Adjointness</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In order for linear operators to be useful for measuring observables, they must satisfy one important property.
            A linear operator <m>A : V \to V</m> is <term>self-adjoint</term> if
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="6in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>\text{dim}(V) =n</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>A = \overline{A}^T</m>. In this context, the <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m> is called <term>Hermitian</term>.
            </p>
            <p>
                You can verify that the matrices 
                <md> A = 
                    \begin{bmatrix}
                    1 \amp 0 \\
                    0 \amp -1 
                    \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
                and
                <md>
                    B=
                    \begin{bmatrix}
                    0 \amp -i \\
                    i \amp 0 
                    \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
                are Hermitian. These are Pauli-z and Pauli-y matrices, respectively, from quantum mechanics.
                
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="6in">
        <statement>
            <p>
               <m>\text{dim}(V)</m> infinite.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                In this case, self-adjoint means
                <md>
                    \langle A[\psi_1(x)], \psi_2(x) \rangle = \langle \psi_1(x), A[\psi_2(x)] \rangle
                </md>.
                This is the correct context for the position, momentum, and energy operators. See the notes from class to verify
                that <m>\hat{x}</m>, <m>\hat{p}</m>, and <m>\hat{H}</m> are all self-adjoint operators.
                
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
    
        
    </exercise>
</page>



       
